WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:02.040
Hi, this is Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.300
How many triangles do you see in this picture?

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:10.240
Give this problem a try, and when your ready, keep watching the video for the solution.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:14.700
We need to count the number of triangles very carefully.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:22.160
We'll first consider triangles which have a size of 4, which means it's side length is equal to 4 times the smallest triangle.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:28.520
There is exactly 1 triangle of this type, and this triangle is facing upward.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:33.000
Let's continue counting only the number of triangles which are facing upward.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.340
Let's count the number of triangles which have size 3.

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:42.660
In the bottom row, there are 2 of these triangles, and 1 row above that there's 1.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:47.240
So that makes for a total of 3 triangles of size 3 facing up.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:51.320
How many triangles are there size 2 facing up?

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:59.200
The bottom row has 3, the next row has 2, and 1 row above that has 1.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.720
3 + 2 + 1 is 6.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:05.600
Finally, we count the number of triangles of size 1 facing up.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:12.720
The bottom row has 4, the next row has 3, the next row has 2, and the top row has 1.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:16.040
4 + 3 + 2 + 1 is equal to 10.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.940
Now we need to count the number of triangles which are facing down.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:24.040
We'll start by counting the number of triangles of size 1.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:31.620
The bottom row has 3, the next row has 2, and the row above that has 1.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:32.280
 

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.940
3 + 2 + 1 is equal to 6.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:41.620
Now, we count the number of triangles of size 2 facing down, and there is exactly 1.

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:46.520
Now, as you can see, this is the largest size triangle that's facing down.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:51.080
So there are no triangles of size 3 or 4 facing down.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:53.180
So we have now listed out all of the triangles

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:58.760
and we can total this up, to get that there are 27 triangles in this picture.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:00.200
 

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:02.500
Now, what would happen if we want to generalize this,

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:04.900
For a base that has 𝘯 smaller triangles?

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:06.280
 

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:07.680
Well we could- We ha- Error.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.780
We found a pattern in the shape we just saw.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.688
The number of triangles facing up of size 𝘯,

00:02:13.688 --> 00:02:15.560
(the largest size), was equal to one

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.340
The next size, there were 2 more of them

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:24.200
The next size there were more of them, according to the shape.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.980
Each time we were adding the next number in the sequence

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.920
These are the triangular numbers which are the shapes

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.400
You can form that have a triangular arrangement of shapes

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.020
and they're equal to the sum of the numbers from one to 𝘯.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:44.920
The triangles of the smallest size, 1, were equal to the triangular number of size 𝘯 .

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:50.160
Now in many videos, we've figured out the way, to sum up the numbers from one to 𝘯

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.160
and this is the triangular formula number,

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.360
which is equal to 𝘯(𝘯+1)/2

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:59.000
to count the number of triangles facing down

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.640
we start from the triangles of size 1

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:04.340
This will be offset by 1

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.940
so we start out with triangular number of 𝘯-1

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:12.480
to get to the next size of 2, we have to offset it by 2

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.700
because each time the row is decreasing in size.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.560
So this is going to go about halfway up

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:23.500
and then the largest two sizes theney the largest size, we're gonna have 0 triangles facing down

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:26.260
so now we've figured out a pattern,

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:28.360
in how to count the number of triangles of each size

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:31.440
and we have a formula for the number of triangular numbers

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:34.680
equal to 𝘯(𝘯+1)/2

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.500
so this actually
going to vary whether you have n equal

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:41.320
𝘯 is going to be even or 𝘯 is going to
be odd

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:45.080
but there's a nice way you can summarize this if you add up all these numbers

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:46.200
the total number of triangles is

00:03:46.209 --> 00:03:52.640
going to be n(n+2) (2n+1)
 all over 8, rounded down.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:54.370
 

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:57.100
this will give you the number of triangles, 
it's actually a sequence

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:03.160
The number of triangles formed if you have this
equilateral grid of triangles the

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:08.760
sequence starts out 1 5 13 27 was what
we had when we had four triangles in our

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:12.880
grid it continues 48 78 and so on.

